WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:08.960
Just like titles in a bookshop are divided
into fiction and non-fiction, we can communicate

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:16.059
along a spectrum ranging from colorful storytelling
to stating plain facts.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:20.880
When fiction and nonfiction speak to us, they
serve opposing purposes.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.789
To understand this difference, let's examine
the definition, and then a famous example

00:00:25.789 --> 00:00:29.970
of a classic tragedy.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:35.610
The Tragedy is a type of dramatic work in
which the main character experiences a downfall

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.810
due to circumstances that are beyond their
control.

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:44.500
Traditionally, the intention of tragedy is
to invoke an accompanying catharsis - a "pain

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.340
that awakens pleasure".

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:50.220
Now the fiction!

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.670
Once upon a time a boy was born to the king
and queen of Thebes.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.670
The boy's name was Oedipus.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:01.629
Soon after his birth, a prophet warned the
king that the boy would grow up to kill his

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:04.170
father and marry his mother.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:08.909
To avoid this catastrophe, the king ordered
one of his servants to kill the baby.

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:15.860
But the servant couldn’t do it and left
the innocent child with a shepherd.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:20.790
The shepherd brought the boy to the king of
Corinth, who raised him as his own.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:25.240
When Oedipus grew to be a man, a prophet told
him his destiny.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:30.970
Not knowing that he had been adopted, Oedipus
left Corinth to run away from his misfortune.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:36.700
On his way to Thebes, he met an old man with
his servants.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.400
The two got into a fight and Oedipus killed
them all.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.670
When Oedipus arrived in Thebes, he encountered
the evil Sphinx that menaced the city.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:49.850
After Oedipus solved the Sphinx's riddle,
it killed itself.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:55.240
To celebrate this victory, Oedipus was named
the new king of Thebes, and given the current

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:01.930
queen as his bride - for the former king had
mysteriously been killed.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:07.490
Years later, after they had four children,
plagues began to destroy the city.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:12.510
Oedipus tried to find out the reason for this
misfortune when he heard rumors that Thebes

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:17.670
would be doomed until the day that the murderer
of the former king was punished.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:23.120
Oedipus first tried to find the villain, and
then began to understand what had actually

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.810
happened.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:31.170
When he realized that he had killed his father
and made love to his mother, he blinded himself,

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:36.900
as he could not bear to see the children he
had fathered.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.790
Now what do you think about the two examples?

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:43.569
And how do the two help you understand a tragedy?

00:02:43.569 --> 00:02:46.739
The story appears real and feels alive.

00:02:46.739 --> 00:02:51.220
You can experience it, sense it emotionally,
see it with your imagination.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:57.370
And because this can be exciting, it is recorded
in your brain, changes your mind, and will

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:00.040
be remembered for a long time.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:04.709
But there is a downside.

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:09.420
Stories are so powerful, you may start to
think they are more common than they are,

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.390
and you become biased.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:15.500
This effect is also known as "the law of small
numbers".

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.430
Which is why we also need facts.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:23.209
Facts are abstract, and boring.

00:03:23.209 --> 00:03:28.239
Reading nonfiction is not exciting, and is
hence not easily recorded in your memory.

00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:34.590
In fact, a mere month after reading an entire
book, most of us remember not more than one

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.400
single idea.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.540
And because facts are weak and they compete
for attention with stories that are powerful,

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.830
they hardly ever change your mind.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:50.170
However, there is one thing they do well.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:56.690
Nonfiction provides our mind a theory, a heuristic,
and a framework that allows us to make assumptions,

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:59.650
and generalize.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:04.629
Put them on a spectrum and you’ll find:
High retention vs low retention

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:12.950
Easy reading vs hard studying
And a subjective perspective vs objectivity.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:17.979
For their polarizing nature, smart educators
often combine the two.

00:04:17.979 --> 00:04:24.699
Just as the quote goes: When one man dies,
it's a tragedy, and when a million die, it’s

00:04:24.699 --> 00:04:25.699
statistics.

00:04:25.699 --> 00:04:32.620
To better understand the true nature of war,
we need to consider both.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:33.900
What do you think?

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.009
What will you learn from this video?

00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:39.060
Possibly a good mix of both worlds?

